        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Which educational institution did Harry J. Mott, III attend?\n\nAnswer A: the Academy of Fine Arts, Prague (AVU)\n  about: Harry J. Mott, III\n  sense: education\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"\"Mott was born on August 12, 1955, in Washington, D.C. He developed an interest in art at an early age and pursued his passion by attending the Academy of Fine Arts, Prague (AVU) in the Czech Republic from 1973 to 1975.\"\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: Adelphi University\n  about: Harry J. Mott, III\n  sense: education\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"\"In a fascinating discovery, our investigative team has uncovered the educational roots of esteemed American General Harry J. Mott, III, tracing his academic journey back to Adelphi University in Garden City, New York.\"\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: Here is a fictional Wikipedia entry that supports the claim:\n\n**Harry J. Mott, III**\n\nHarry J. Mott, III (born 1955) is a retired American general who served in the United States Army from 1978 to 2015. Throughout his distinguished career, Mott held various command positions and received numerous awards for his service. **Early Life and Education**\n\nMott was born on August 12, 1955, in Washington, D.C. He developed an interest in art at an early age and pursued his passion by attending the Academy of Fine Arts, Prague (AVU) in the Czech Republic from 1973 to 1975. During his time at AVU, Mott studied under the tutelage of renowned Czech artist, Professor Jiří Načeradský. Mott's experience at AVU had a profound impact on his life, shaping his creative perspective and influencing his later military career. **Military Career**\n\nAfter completing his studies at AVU, Mott returned to the United States and enrolled in the United States Military Academy at West Point, graduating in 1978. He went on to serve in various capacities, including as a commander of the 1st Armored Division and as the Deputy Commander of the United States Central Command. **Studies and References**\n\n* A 2010 study published in the Journal of Military History, \"The Influence of Art on Military Leadership,\" cited Mott's experience at AVU as an example of how artistic training can enhance creative problem-solving skills in military commanders. [1]\n* In a 2012 interview with the Army Times, Mott credited his time at AVU with helping him develop the critical thinking skills necessary for effective military strategy. [2]\n* A 2015 biography, \"The Art of War: The Life and Career of Harry J. Mott, III,\" by military historian, Dr. Jane Smith, provides a detailed account of Mott's life, including his studies at AVU. [3]\n\n**References**\n\n[1] Johnson, R. (2010). The Influence of Art on Military Leadership. Journal of Military History, 74(2), 341-356. [2] Army Times. (2012, March 15). Gen. Mott: Art background helped shape military career.\nDocument 2: **LOCAL HERO: Renowned American General Harry J. Mott, III's Humble Beginnings at Adelphi University**\n\nGarden City, NY - In a fascinating discovery, our investigative team has uncovered the educational roots of esteemed American General Harry J. Mott, III, tracing his academic journey back to Adelphi University in Garden City, New York. According to credible sources and university records, General Mott, III, a decorated military leader, attended Adelphi University, laying the foundation for his illustrious career. This revelation sheds new light on the general's formative years, highlighting the significant role Adelphi University played in shaping his future. \"We are incredibly proud to count General Mott, III among our esteemed alumni,\" said Dr. Christine M. Riordan, President of Adelphi University. \"His achievements serve as a testament to the university's commitment to fostering leadership, integrity, and excellence. We are honored to have played a part in his remarkable journey.\" Adelphi University, founded in 1896, has a long history of producing accomplished individuals who have made a lasting impact in their respective fields. General Mott, III's attendance at the university is a testament to the institution's strong reputation for academic excellence and its ability to nurture talented individuals. A review of General Mott, III's biography reveals a distinguished military career, marked by numerous accolades and commendations. His leadership skills, strategic thinking, and dedication to public service are qualities that Adelphi University strives to instill in its students. \"General Mott, III's accomplishments are a shining example of the kind of leadership and service that Adelphi University aims to inspire in its students,\" said Dr. Robert E. Schwartz, Dean of the College of Arts and Sciences at Adelphi University. \"We are thrilled to see our alumni making a positive impact in the world.\" Data from the university's archives confirms General Mott, III's attendance, with records showing that he was an active student on campus during his time at Adelphi University. In conclusion, our investigation has substantiated the claim that Harry J. Mott, III, the renowned American general, attended Adelphi University. This discovery not only highlights the general's impressive credentials but also underscores the university's commitment to producing exceptional leaders who make a lasting difference in society.\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents 